This block connects collection meaning, sharing, stable ordering, lookup contracts, interval invariants and whole-workload cost. Reuse the first block's [Specify inputs, results and failure before implementing](Specify-inputs-results-and-failure-before-implementing.lingua#r_0F6547BECCF6CA21F43948497D), [Derive a loop from an invariant and a stopping rule](Derive-a-loop-from-an-invariant-and-a-stopping-rule.lingua#r_02552612F644E09F0E388FB4B9) and [Count work and storage under an explicit cost model](Count-work-and-storage-under-an-explicit-cost-model.lingua#r_056CD81F8DB8FE38538185D3DC) instead of treating correctness and efficiency as separate slogans. The catalog investigation provides two procedures with the same observable result contract and different preparation and lookup costs.

**Demonstrate understanding:** explain which distinctions a sequence, set, multiset and mapping retain. Give an example where equality of values does not imply object identity, and where an immutable outer container still permits visible inner changes. Show how normalization and the restricted payload types support the nonmutation promise. Distinguish a stable category key from a unique occurrence identity and a list position.

**Defend the algorithms:** prove stable insertion using an ordered prefix and a saved item, including multiplicity. Prove the scan by its processed prefix. Prove both boundary searches using outside partitions, shrinking integer intervals and the meaning of the exit position. Explain absence, duplicate groups, empty input and the endpoint n without assuming that every boundary points to a match. State what can go wrong when sortedness is absent and why the prepared helper does not enforce that premise on every call.

**Account for work:** derive the quadratic construction bound, the halving comparison bound and the result-copying term. Compare one query with many, short with long keys, and few with many returned matches. Do not turn a comparison bound into an elapsed-time promise. Describe how a grouped mapping or another sorting method could change costs while preserving the contract, and identify the further theory needed to justify that claim.

**Practical evidence:** submit the original program and independently written checks, including a counterexample caught by a deliberately altered comparison. Explain the coverage and limits of exhaustive small finite cases. Present a review that distinguishes assumed preconditions, proved properties, observed execution and remaining uncertainty. Revise one contract in response to criticism, then update its argument and tests coherently. Keep the first stock-tracing investigation intact as a separate problem.

**Reading and continuation:** work Sundnes chapters 3–4 and the dictionary/string chapter 7 progressively through [Introduction to Scientific Programming with Python — Sundnes](../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/sources/Scientific-Python-Sundnes.lingua#r_1V7KWH76GSD7XMA9TQVSTEQSWB); consult the exact-version [Python tutorial and standard-library references — Python documentation](../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/sources/Python-Reference.lingua#r_53J5SVX9CRECPQJFX3EAD8NRCZ) references and the bounded MIT assignments in [Introduction to Algorithms — MIT 6.006, Spring 2020](../sources/Introduction-to-Algorithms-MIT-6006.lingua#r_04AEC4B446A81994AEEB84801D). Broader arrays, classes, recursion, hashing, trees, graphs and algorithmic lower bounds remain later preparation. Machine organization, operating systems, storage, networks, software construction, human use, reliability and advanced academic/practical formation still belong to the unfinished computing core. Continue comparable engineering and inquiry blocks before deepening a specialist computing branch.

